01:11:00,000 --> 01:11:06,000
The Killing Fields is a 1984 British biographical drama film about the Khmer Rouge regime in Cambodia, which is
based on the experiences of two journalists, Cambodian Dith Pran and American Sydney Schanberg.

14
01:16:27,692 --> 01:16:33,692
It was directed by Roland Joffé and produced by
David Puttnam for Goldcrest Films.

15
01:21:55,384 --> 01:22:01,384
Sam Waterston stars as Schanberg, Haing S.

16
01:27:23,076 --> 01:27:29,076
Ngor as Pran, and John Malkovich as Al Rockoff.

17
01:32:50,769 --> 01:32:56,769
The adaptation for the screen was written by Bruce Robinson; the musical score was written by Mike
Oldfield and orchestrated by David Bedford; and the costumes were designed by Judy Moorcroft.

18
01:38:18,461 --> 01:38:24,461
The film was a success at the box office and an instant hit with critics.

19
01:43:46,153 --> 01:43:52,153
At the 57th Academy Awards it received seven Oscar nominations, including Best
Picture; it won three, most notably Best Supporting Actor for Haing S.

20
01:49:13,846 --> 01:49:19,846
Ngor, who had no previous acting experience, as well as
Best Cinematography and Best Editing.

21
01:54:41,538 --> 01:54:47,538
At the 38th British Academy Film Awards, it won eight BAFTAs, including
Best Film and Best Actor in a Leading Role for Ngor.

22
02:00:09,230 --> 02:00:15,230
In 1999, the British Film Institute voted The Killing Fields the
100th-greatest British film of the 20th century.

23
02:05:36,923 --> 02:05:42,923
It is now seen as one of the greatest films of all time.

24
